      "updated_at": "2026-07-01T11:48:44Z",
      "body": "Thanks for calling this out! We're taking a look but some background might be helpful for you, and some clarification might be helpful for us.\n\nThis is a little bit of an unexpected workflow which is why this wasn't more broadly announced in our changelog. The change came about here https://github.com/rerun-io/rerun/commit/483370692c187865c9a694264897a5d4e02a5166\n\nHere's a bit more background. RRDs now have footers which dramatically improve performance (especially if querying against the RRD), however, the existing model is that the footer isn't added until writing is completed. We were relying on an explicit call to `disconnect` on the stream to denote that it is done being used. However, that was easy to miss so lots of people were ending up with RRDs without footers and paying a major performance penalty. In the linked PR we finalize the recording when the context manager is exited if using a file sink to make this more automated.\n\n```python\nwith rr.RecordingStream(\"repro\") as stream:\n   stream.save(\"/tmp/repro.rrd\")\n   stream.set_time(\"frame\", sequence=i)\n   stream.log(\"img\", rr.Image(np.zeros((4, 4, 3), np.uint8)))\n# copy /tmp/repro.rrd somewhere would move the file before the footer was written before\n```\n\nCan you provide a bit more context for the shape of your use case? I'm not clear on why you would construct a stream then repeatedly re-enter the context manager instead of just doing stateful logging (the context manage is equivalent just through the indirection of storing/checking/removing the global stream).\n\n```python\nstream = rr.RecordingStream(\"repro\")\nstream.save(\"/tmp/repro.rrd\")\nfor i in range(5):\n    stream.set_time(\"frame\", sequence=i)\n    stream.log(\"img\", rr.Image(np.zeros((4, 4, 3), np.uint8)))\n```",
      "author_association": "MEMBER",
      "pin": null,
