      "created_at": "2026-07-02T07:51:52Z",
      "updated_at": "2026-07-02T07:51:52Z",
      "body": "Thank you for looking into this and for the clarification. I now understand better why it was changed like this.\n\nRegarding your question about my use case: I have an image processing pipeline that processes incoming frames sequentially. Each frame contains multiple objects and for each object it executes a set of processing steps. These processing steps log data to a per-object rrd file. So the actual loop looks roughly like this\n\n```\nfor each frame:\n  for each object: \n    with object.stream:\n      set_time(frame)\n      deep_call_tree()\n        \u2514\u2500 rr.log(...)\n```\n\nI can implement my own context manager or refactor my code to make it work again.\n\nIs there a valid use case to use an already finalized recording with a context manager? If not, could you throw an exception in this case? ",
